Difference Between .NET Framework, .NET, .NET Core:
•	The name may be confusing .NET Framework, .NET Core, .NET(Modern .NET) are related, but represents different versions / generations of Microsoft Development platform
•	.NET Framework (2002) ──────────────► Legacy Windows-only
•	.NET Core (2016) ──────────────► Cross-platform, open-source
•	.NET 5 (2020)
•	.NET 6 (2021 LTS)
•	.NET 7 (2022)
•	.NET 8 (2023 LTS)
•	.NET 9 (2024)
•	.NET 10 (2025)
•	      ▲
•	      └── Unified platform (formerly .NET Core)
.NET Framework:
•	The Original Microsoft framework released in 2002
Features:
•	Runs only on Windows
•	Supports ASP.NET MVC 5, Web Forms, WCF, WPF, Windows Forms
•	Latest version of .NET Framework is 4.8.1
.NET Core
•	Released in 2016 as a redesign of .NET Framework
Why Microsoft Created it:
•	To overcome the limitation in .NET Framework such as Windows-only, Large Installation footprint, difficult to deploy independently, not ideal for containers and cloud
Features:
•	Cross Platform (Windows, Linux, macOS)
•	Open Source
•	High Performance compared to .NET Framework
•	Container-friendly
•	Cloud-ready
•	ASP.NET Core 2.1, 3.1, Console Applications, Microservices, REST APIs
•	Latest version of .NET Core is 1.0, 2.0, 3.1
Modern .NET (.NET 5+):
•	Microsoft renamed .NET Core into a single platform called .NET
Features:
•	Cross-Platform
•	Open Source
•	High Performance
•	Cloud-native
•	Supports Web, Desktop, Mobile, IoT, Microservices, APIs
•	One Platform for all Application types
•	ASP.NET Core Web API, Blazor, MAUI, Worker Services, Microservices, Console Apps
•	Current popular version is .NET 8, latest is .NET 10
Feature	.NET Framework	.NET Core	Modern .NET
First Release	2002	2016	2020 (.NET 5)
Platform	Windows Only	Cross-platform	Cross-platform
Open Source	No	Yes	Yes
Performance	Good	Better	Best
Cloud Support	Limited	Excellent	Excellent
Docker Support	No	Yes	Yes
Microservices	Difficult	Excellent	Excellent
ASP.NET	ASP.NET MVC/Web Forms	ASP.NET Core	ASP.NET Core
Future Development	Maintenance Only	Discontinued	Active Development
Latest Version	4.8.1	3.1	.NET 10
•	.NET Framework uses CLR and .NET & .NET(Modern .NET) uses CoreCLR



The interviewer may ask What are the difference between .Net framework & .NET Core? OR What are the advantages of .NET Core over .NET Framework?
•	The first advantage is .NET core is Cross Platform, it means it can run on Windows, Linux, macOS rather .NET Framework only be run on Windows
•	Second advantage of .NET Core is it is Completely Open Source, rather .NET framework is paid means company had to purchase the license to run .NET framework-based applications on their servers (for students it is free not for companies)
•	Third one is Hosting. .NET Core can be hosted in IIS, Kestrel, Nginx rather .NET Framework is only can be hosted in IIS
•	Another advantage of .NET Core is Built-in dependency injection which enable the application loosely coupled, Reuseable, Testable rather in .NET Framework achieving DI is complex
•	Last Advantage is .NET Core supports multiple IDEs such as VS, Vs for Mac, VS Code rather .Net Framework only supports VS 
